#. type: Title #
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Error suppression and tailoring {#error-suppression-and-tailoring}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Error suppression can refer to any technique that anticipates and tries to "
"avoid certain types of noise and errors. This is easiest to explain through "
"concrete examples, but keep in mind that these methods are not limited to "
"the examples shown here, and new methods are continually being explored. "
"Sometimes it is not possible to suppress errors, but it is possible to "
"affect how they accumulate. If we can make errors accumulate more slowly, we"
" might describe this as suppressing the overall error, but it is more "
"accurately viewed as noise tailoring. In this lesson we cover canonical "
"examples of error suppression (dynamical decoupling) and of noise "
"tailoring/shaping (Pauli twirling).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A video to accompany this lesson will launch in the next few days, and will "
"be embedded here.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Dynamical decoupling {#dynamical-decoupling}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Let us begin with a very simple state on a single qubit, and introduce a "
"simple noise model. This is insufficient to completely explain dynamical "
"decoupling, but it gives us a clear example. Suppose we have a qubit "
"prepared in a superposition state using a Hadamard gate:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\psi\\rangle = H|0\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left( |0\\rangle +|1\\rangle \\right).\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This state is visualized on the Bloch sphere as shown below on the left. In "
"the ideal case with zero noise, this qubit would remain in this state until "
"the next operation is carried out. However, we know from the previous lesson"
" that this is not what we observe. Noise causes the quantum information to "
"degrade.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![A Bloch sphere with a state vector initially equal to the plus state. The "
"state then precesses around the z axis while remaining in the xy plane, "
"consistent with time evolution in the presence of a magnetic field oriented "
"along the z axis.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-"
"advantage/error-suppression/precess.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Noise or environmental coupling can cause the relative phase between the "
"basis states to change. That is, the probabilities of $|0\\rangle$ and "
"$|1\\rangle$ do not change, as the absolute values of their coefficients do "
"not change. Rather, the phases of the amplitudes change, altering their real"
" and imaginary components. To make this discussion more concrete, let us "
"consider one type of interaction that can cause this: coupling to a magnetic"
" field oriented in the Z direction: $\\vec{B} = (0,0,B_0)$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Consider what happens to the state $|+\\rangle$ as time passes:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\psi(t)\\rangle = e^{-iHt/\\hbar}|\\psi(0)\\rangle \\rightarrow e^{-iZ\\omega_B t/2}|\\psi(0)\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here we have used the fact that a magnetic field in the Z direction causes "
"precession about the Z axis with a frequency $\\omega_B$ that depends on the"
" effective magnetic moment and magnetic field strength. The details are less"
" important than the fact that this interaction causes evolution about the Z "
"axis, resulting in opposite phase accumulation for the two computational "
"basis states. Applying this operator to each term in $|\\psi\\rangle$, we "
"find the following:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\psi(t)\\rangle = e^{-iZ\\omega_B t/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left( |0\\rangle +|1\\rangle \\right)\n"
"=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left( e^{-i\\omega_B t/2}|0\\rangle +e^{i\\omega_B t/2}|1\\rangle \\right)\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This time-dependent phase corresponds to precession about the Z axis in the "
"Bloch sphere picture. This is shown in the right half of the figure above.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If we knew that this interaction were occurring in a controlled way, we "
"could predict it. To model this type of noise, we instead consider a "
"distribution of possible magnetic field strengths, each occurring with some "
"classical probability (other sources of noise would need to be modeled "
"differently). We need to consider what happens when there is a non-zero "
"classical probability that the state has not rotated at all, and also some "
"probability that it has rotated by a small amount, or even by a large "
"amount. This distribution of possible rotations is the reason for the "
"spreading of the state in the Bloch sphere picture below. If there is more "
"such dephasing noise, the phase will be less well-defined. In the limit of "
"strong dephasing, the qubit becomes completely dephased, corresponding to "
"the loss of the quantum coherence stored in the state.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Three panels, each showing a Bloch sphere. The first shows a pure quantum "
"state vector, the plus state. The second shows a broadened, shorter region "
"of state space, indicating a mixed state with imperfect phase information. "
"The third shows a tiny region near the origin corresponding to complete "
"dephasing.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-"
"suppression/dephasing.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Of course, in an experiment we have no idea what random coupling will occur."
" What can we do about this?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Suppose that the environmental coupling remains approximately constant over "
"a time interval $2t_0$. Consider what would happen if we followed this "
"prescription:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Allow the phase to change for time $t_0$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Apply an X gate to the qubit\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Allow the same environmental coupling to occur for a further time $t_0$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Apply a second X gate\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"After the initial time evolution, we would have exactly the state above. "
"Applying the first X gate we have:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"X|\\psi(t_0)\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left( e^{-i\\omega_B t_0/2}|1\\rangle +e^{i\\omega_B t_0/2}|0\\rangle \\right)\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now when the second interval of $t_0$ passes, the same interaction takes "
"place. But now the amplitudes are associated with the opposite Z "
"eigenstates, meaning the sign of the rotation about the Z axis has switched."
" After allowing the system to evolve in time for another $t_0$ we have the "
"state:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"e^{-iZ\\omega_B (t-t_0)/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left( e^{-i\\omega_B t_0/2}|1\\rangle +e^{i\\omega_B t_0/2}|0\\rangle \\right)\n"
"=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left( e^{-i\\omega_B (t-t_0)/2}e^{i\\omega_B t_0/2}|1\\rangle +e^{i\\omega_B (t-t_0)/2}e^{-i\\omega_B t_0/2}|0\\rangle \\right)\n"
"$$\n"
"And inserting $t = 2t_0$ we have:\n"
"$$\n"
"|\\psi(2t_0)\\rangle=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left(|1\\rangle +|0\\rangle \\right)\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Applying the final X gate does nothing in this case, but it is generally "
"necessary:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\psi(2t_0)\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left(|0\\rangle +|1\\rangle \\right) = |\\psi(t=0)\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We have recovered the original quantum state, including its relative phase. "
"This process is an especially simple example of dynamical decoupling.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"More generally, __dynamical decoupling__ (DD) involves inserting some "
"single-qubit gates to reduce the effect of interactions with systems outside"
" the qubit (decoupling it from the environment). The complete refocusing of "
"all possible phase histories back to the original state, as shown above, is "
"somewhat idealized, but it is still a possible scenario. Let's discuss when "
"DD is useful and what caveats exist.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In the text above, we stepped through the effect of an `XX` DD sequence on "
"the initial state $|+\\rangle$. Check whether the same steps above also "
"return the state $|+i\\rangle$ back to its initial state under the same "
"assumptions of a slowly-varying magnetic field along the z direction.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\psi(t)\\rangle = e^{-iZ\\omega_B t/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left( |0\\rangle +i|1\\rangle \\right)\n"
"=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left( e^{-i\\omega_B t/2}|0\\rangle +i e^{i\\omega_B t/2}|1\\rangle \\right)\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"X|\\psi(t_0)\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left( e^{-i\\omega_B t_0/2}|1\\rangle +i e^{i\\omega_B t_0/2}|0\\rangle \\right)\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"e^{-iZ\\omega_B (t-t_0)/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left( e^{-i\\omega_B t_0/2}|1\\rangle +i e^{i\\omega_B t_0/2}|0\\rangle \\right)\n"
"=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left( e^{-i\\omega_B (t-t_0)/2}e^{i\\omega_B t_0/2}|1\\rangle +i e^{i\\omega_B (t-t_0)/2}e^{-i\\omega_B t_0/2}|0\\rangle \\right)\n"
"$$\n"
"And inserting $t = 2t_0$ we have\n"
"$$\n"
"|\\psi(2t_0)\\rangle=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left(|1\\rangle +i |0\\rangle \\right)\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Applying the final X gate, we have:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\psi(2t_0)\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left(|0\\rangle +i |1\\rangle \\right) = |\\psi(t=0)\\rangle = |+i\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When to use DD {#when-to-use-dd}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The first and most obvious caveat is that we assumed a purely dephasing "
"channel in our treatment. Real-world interactions typically produce a "
"mixture of dephasing and other error mechanisms. In the treatment above, we "
"chose to apply X gates specifically. This is referred to as an `XX` sequence"
" in dynamical decoupling. This particular sequence is appropriate for purely"
" dephasing errors. But there are other sequences that might be more broadly "
"applicable, like `XY4` (shown in the circuit diagram below) and the more "
"complex `XY8`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![A quantum circuit showing a sequence of four quantum gates used in "
"dynamical decoupling: an X gate, a Y gate, a second X gate, and a second Y "
"gate.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-"
"suppression/xy4.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Another caveat is that dynamical decoupling adds single-qubit gates, which "
"can add single-qubit errors due to gate imperfections or even crosstalk. "
"These single-qubit error rates are typically much lower than multi-qubit "
"gate errors, so this is usually not a major concern, but it is something to "
"keep in mind if many qubits are employing DD many times throughout your "
"circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"DD is useful when the external coupling has time to affect the state of the "
"qubit. Because qubits are painstakingly well isolated and external couplings"
" should be weak, this type of noise is most noticeable when a qubit sits "
"idle for long periods. For short idle times, the effect of DD could be to "
"add single-qubit gate errors while suppressing very little noise; the "
"fidelity of your circuit could actually be reduced.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"__Key takeaway__: Use dynamical decoupling when qubits remain idle for "
"sufficiently long periods, and pay attention to the type of DD sequence "
"being used.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Dynamical decoupling using Qiskit {#dynamical-decoupling-using-qiskit}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Let us explore the use of DD by examining the case of several qubits "
"prepared in the $|+\\rangle$ state, which then remain idle for a long time. "
"In the absence of errors, preparing the multi-qubit state $|\\psi\\rangle = "
"|+\\rangle^{\\otimes N}$ and then applying a Hadamard gate before "
"measurement (thereby measuring in the X basis) should always yield 0. With "
"noise, the measurement outcome will be 0 only a fraction of the time rather "
"than with 100% probability. We calculate the average `X` expectation value "
"across multiple qubits. That is, we are interested in the following:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"f\\equiv \\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}{\\langle\\Psi | X_j |\\Psi\\rangle}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"where $X_j \\equiv III..X...II$ with the `X` operator in the $j^{th}$ "
"position from the right and $|\\Psi\\rangle$ is the state of the entire "
"system.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This circuit contains intentional delays, which is somewhat contrived for a "
"benchmark. However, it is very common for real circuits to contain qubits "
"that sit idle during part of the execution. You can think of this as a "
"simplified model of a more complex circuit in which some qubits remain idle "
"for part of the computation. This type of benchmarking based on the "
"evolution of $|+\\rangle$ states is often referred to as _Ramsey "
"benchmarking_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We begin by loading the necessary packages and configuring the service.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now we define some helper functions. First, we want to address the point "
"about long idle times. What exactly does \"long\" mean in this context? We "
"could simply report the idle time in microseconds. However, it is useful to "
"know how many two-qubit gates could be executed during that same interval. "
"This makes the idle times more directly indicative of the circuit depth that"
" could have been executed during the same period. The first helper function "
"gets the two-qubit gate native to the backend and determines the duration of"
" that gate.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The second function simply creates a Ramsey circuit (one with qubits in the "
"$|+\\rangle$ state), implements a delay, rotates using an H gate, and then "
"measures. Recall that a Hadamard gate (H) maps $|+\\rangle$ to $|0\\rangle$,"
" so measuring $|0\\rangle$ in the `Z` basis after the Hadamard corresponds "
"to the qubit having been in $|+\\rangle$ immediately before the Hadamard.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Our final function converts the raw counts of 0 and 1 measurement outcomes "
"into an expectation value of `X`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now we specify the details of our test, including the number of qubits and "
"the gate sequence to be used in DD (in this case `XX`). Note especially that"
" we set the optimization level to zero. In practice, you would often select "
"a higher optimization level; here we use level 0 to ensure that the effects "
"of the errors targeted by DD remain visible. Finally, we determine the "
"characteristic time for two-qubit gates on this backend and print some "
"relevant times.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We now construct our circuits and transpile them for our chosen backend.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We should visualize at least one circuit to ensure we have coded our circuit"
" with the desired states and delays. It might be easier to visualize the "
"pre-transpiled circuit `qc`, or you might check the transpiled one "
"`qc_isa`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-"
"quantum-advantage/error-suppression/extracted-outputs/48a7cd69-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Execute {#execute}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We're finally ready to execute on hardware. We use Sampler to obtain many "
"measurements of each qubit, and we will use it twice: once with DD "
"explicitly turned off, and once with DD turned on and using the `XX` gate "
"sequence.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "We can extract the counts from the various circuits.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Post-processing {#post-processing}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We now have the counts from the measurement, but we want to turn that into "
"the expectation value of `X` and then average those expectation values "
"across all the qubits we used, to learn about the preservation of phase "
"information. For that we use our `counts_to_x_expectations` function defined"
" earlier.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Finally, let's plot our results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-"
"quantum-advantage/error-suppression/extracted-outputs/f406833c-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As you can see, with no explicit delay the expectation values are indeed "
"near 1, which we would expect if all phase information were preserved. If "
"all phase information were lost, there would be no preference for the final "
"rotation to produce $|0\\rangle$ rather than $|1\\rangle$, and the average "
"expectation value would approach zero. In the data, we see that as delay "
"times increase, the average expectation value of `X` decreases, starting to "
"approach zero. Note that DD has been very effective here; the expectation "
"values with DD are typically more than 20% better (closer to 1) than the "
"values without DD. But also note that the first delayed data point "
"corresponds to a delay roughly equivalent to 100 two-qubit gate operations. "
"This reinforces the point that DD is most useful when qubits remain idle for"
" relatively long periods.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-1}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If we apply DD using `XY4` to the same circuit as before, do you expect it "
"to yield results that are much better, much worse, or about the same, as DD "
"using `XX`? Explain.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"About the same, perhaps slightly worse. The circuit we used had states "
"rotated into the XY plane. This state stores information primarily in its "
"phase, making it especially sensitive to dephasing errors rather than T1 "
"relaxation. XY4 might help with a wider variety of errors, but XX is already"
" optimized to help the circuit we are using. XY4 might be just as good, but "
"would not add anything substantial, or the fact that XY4 contains more gates"
" might allow additional gate errors to make the results slightly worse.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The last result used the simplest DD gate sequence `XX`. Let's see how to "
"implement a more complex sequence, `XY4`. We define a Sampler in the next "
"section.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-"
"quantum-advantage/error-suppression/extracted-outputs/c830c2ce-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here we see that `XY4` is not appreciably different from `XX`. It might be "
"very slightly worse due to additional gates in the `XY4` sequence, but more "
"importantly, we already explained why `XX` would have the desired effect in "
"preserving phase specifically for a state like $|+\\rangle$. There is no "
"reason to think that for such an initial state, a different sequence would "
"improve results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-2}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Verify that the `XY4` sequence leaves the state unchanged up to a global "
"phase.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"YXYX|+\\rangle & = YXYX(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)\\\\\n"
"& = YXY(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)\\\\\n"
"& = YX(i|1\\rangle-i|0\\rangle)\\\\\n"
"& = Y(i|0\\rangle-i|1\\rangle)\\\\\n"
"& = (-|0\\rangle-|1\\rangle)\\\\\n"
"& =-|+\\rangle\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Pauli twirling {#pauli-twirling}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We should begin by noting that Pauli twirling is often used not as an error-"
"suppression technique, but as an error-shaping technique: it makes "
"noise/errors behave differently, sometimes more predictably, to enable other"
" methods. Although Pauli twirling does not *prevent* errors, it might "
"prevent their coherent accumulation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In a quantum circuit, multiple different sources of error add together. The "
"errors can add in different ways, notably _coherently_ and _incoherently_. "
"Coherent accumulation of errors means that noise or imperfect "
"implementations all tend to drive errors in the same direction across "
"multiple layers and gates. An example would be coherent over-rotation when "
"applying a rotation gate.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Consider an ideal rotation gate, such as $R_x(\\theta_0)$, which rotates "
"about the X axis by exactly $\\theta_0$. Of course, gate implementation is "
"not perfect, and the actual rotation might be $\\theta_0+\\Delta\\theta$ for"
" one implementation, and it could even be the case that $\\Delta\\theta$ is "
"always the same sign, and possibly similar in magnitude across many "
"applications of $R_x(\\theta)$. Thus repeated application of rotation gates "
"can result in coherent accumulation of these many over-rotations (or under-"
"rotations), $\\Delta \\theta$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Incoherent error accumulation is just the opposite: errors in random "
"directions with random signs, such that errors in different layers do not "
"always interfere additively, but sometimes cancel or add in quadrature. "
"Clearly incoherent errors accumulate more slowly in terms of the overall "
"effect on the state of the qubit. A toy diagram of this is shown in the "
"figure below. This is a simplification. Real quantum errors are not "
"restricted to a two-dimensional Cartesian space; not all error contributions"
" will have the same magnitude, and there are more complexities. But the "
"intuition of a picture like this is useful: coherent errors tend to "
"accumulate faster than incoherent ones.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Two images. First, vectors denoting errors arranged in a line such that "
"they add up coherently to a large error. Second, vectors in random "
"directions being added to yield a smaller net effect, as in incoherent error"
" accumulation.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-"
"suppression/accumulation.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"One can often obtain higher-fidelity results by turning coherent error "
"accumulation into incoherent error accumulation. A primary way of "
"accomplishing this is called __Pauli twirling__.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Pauli twirling refers to adding combinations of Pauli gates $P \\in \\{X, Y,"
" Z, I\\}$ before and after a desired gate operation $U$ in such a way that "
"$P_1 U P_2 = U$. Here, $P_1$ and $P_2$ are not single Paulis, but "
"collections of Pauli operators often acting on multiple qubits. You might "
"sometimes see it stated that the action of the extra Pauli gates is "
"\"equivalent to the identity\". But this is imprecise and potentially "
"confusing. The Pauli gates are separated by $U$ and the goal is to leave the"
" logical action of all gates equal to $U$. Sometimes $U$ is called the "
"\"payload\" to distinguish this intended operation from the gates added for "
"suppression. Some examples of Pauli twirling around a CNOT gate are shown "
"below.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Four images showing pieces of four quantum circuits, each with two qubits."
" The first is a simple CNOT gate. The others each show a CNOT gate but "
"surrounded by Pauli gates in a way that preserves the overall logic of a "
"CNOT operation.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-"
"suppression/twirl-4.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Let's walk through just one example to verify that twirling leaves the logical effect of the payload unchanged.\n"
"Without loss of generality, let the two-qubit states involving $q_0$ and $q_1$ be:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\psi_\\text{init}\\rangle = a|00\\rangle + b|01\\rangle + c|10\\rangle + d|11\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As always, we are using the qubit ordering convention $|q_1,q_0\\rangle$. "
"Applying a CX gate with $q_1$ as the target yields\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"CX|\\psi_\\text{init}\\rangle = |\\psi_\\text{final}\\rangle = a|00\\rangle + b|11\\rangle + c|10\\rangle + d|01\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now let us consider the third circuit shown, using X gates for Pauli "
"twirling. We could simply multiply the matrices together and verify that "
"they yield a CNOT matrix. Alternatively, we can track the operation on an "
"arbitrary quantum state through the circuit, as we do below. The states at "
"different points in the circuit have been labeled a-d.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![A CNOT operation on a two-qubit quantum circuit, surrounded by three X "
"gates. Points are labeled a-d at the beginning of the circuit, after an X "
"gate on the control qubit, after the CNOT gate, and after a final X gate is "
"applied to each of the two qubits, "
"respectively.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-"
"suppression/twirl-steps.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\psi_a\\rangle = a|00\\rangle + b|01\\rangle + c|10\\rangle + d|11\\rangle\\\\\n"
"|\\psi_b\\rangle = a|01\\rangle + b|00\\rangle + c|11\\rangle + d|10\\rangle\\\\\n"
"|\\psi_c\\rangle = a|11\\rangle + b|00\\rangle + c|01\\rangle + d|10\\rangle\\\\\n"
"|\\psi_d\\rangle = a|00\\rangle + b|11\\rangle + c|10\\rangle + d|01\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This is exactly $|\\psi_\\text{final}\\rangle$ that we obtained before with "
"no twirling. Indeed, this twirled gate sequence leaves the logical action of"
" the payload unchanged. However, if different valid twirling sequences are "
"selected randomly from layer to layer, coherent error accumulation can be "
"converted into effectively stochastic (incoherent) error accumulation. To be"
" clear, one does not choose a single twirling pattern and use it throughout "
"the circuit. Instead, different valid twirling sequences are selected for "
"different layers. An example with many entangling layers might look like "
"this.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![A quantum circuit with four qubits and three CNOT gates in a ladder "
"arrangement. In twirling, each of these CNOT gates is surrounded by a "
"different set of Pauli gates.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-"
"advantage/error-suppression/twirl-mult.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-3}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Verify that the Pauli twirling in the fourth panel in the figure above also "
"leaves the logical effect of the CNOT unchanged.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We follow the example above and show that the action on any arbitrary two-"
"qubit state is equivalent to a CNOT operation. We will refer to the labeled "
"points in this diagram.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![A quantum circuit with two qubits. The initial state is labeled \"a\", "
"then an X gate acts on qubit 0 and a Y gate acts on qubit 1. After that, the"
" state is labeled \"b\". Then a CNOT gate acts with qubit 0 as the control "
"and qubit 1 as the target. After that, CNOT gate the state is labeled \"c\"."
" Finally, a Y gate acts on qubit 0 and a Z gate acts on qubit 1. The final "
"state is labeled \"d\".](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-"
"advantage/error-suppression/twirl-check.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"|\\psi_a\\rangle & = a|00\\rangle + b|01\\rangle + c|10\\rangle + d|11\\rangle\\\\\n"
"|\\psi_b\\rangle & = ai|11\\rangle + bi|10\\rangle - ci|01\\rangle - di|00\\rangle\\\\\n"
"|\\psi_c\\rangle & = ai|01\\rangle + bi|10\\rangle - ci|11\\rangle - di|00\\rangle\\\\\n"
"|\\psi_d\\rangle & = ai(+1)(-i)|00\\rangle + bi(-1)(i)|11\\rangle - ci(-1)(-i)|10\\rangle - di(+1)(i)|01\\rangle\\\\\n"
"|\\psi_d\\rangle & = a|00\\rangle + b|11\\rangle + c|10\\rangle + d|01\\rangle\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This is equivalent to the action of a CNOT with qubit 0 as the control and "
"qubit 1 as the target.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Can you come up with a Pauli twirling sequence for the CNOT gate which is "
"not shown above?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Yes, there are many others. One example is $Z_t$ before the CNOT, and a "
"$Z_t$ and $Z_c$ after the CNOT.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When to use Pauli twirling {#when-to-use-pauli-twirling}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As presented here, Pauli twirling is applied only to multi-qubit gates. "
"Applying a similar protocol to single-qubit gates would require different "
"logic and is generally not useful in practice. Pauli twirling itself uses "
"several single-qubit gates (the Pauli gates). The additional Pauli gates "
"would likely introduce more error than would be gained by randomizing any "
"coherent error accumulation. Error rates associated with two-qubit gates are"
" much larger than those associated with single-qubit gates. Further, some "
"single-qubit gates are non-Clifford, which cannot be fully twirled. This is "
"why Qiskit includes Pauli twirling options that automatically twirl around "
"two-qubit gates, and not around single-qubit gates.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This was implicit in the figure above: Pauli twirling was implemented around"
" the CX gates, but not around the Hadamard gate.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Let's see two examples of Pauli twirling in action.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Pauli twirling to suppress coherent accumulation {#pauli-twirling-to-"
"suppress-coherent-accumulation}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To observe how Pauli twirling can turn coherent error accumulation into "
"slower incoherent accumulation, we want a circuit and observable that "
"function as a coherent-error stress test. The sole purpose is to make "
"coherently accumulating two‑qubit errors visible, and then show how Pauli "
"twirling converts that coherent buildup into stochastic decay.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"CNOT (or CZ) gates are a common source of coherent errors. The simplest "
"experiment we can do in this case is to initialize a state (say "
"$|+\\rangle$), apply layers of paired CNOT gates (using the fact that two "
"CNOTs yield an identity), and check how errors accumulate as the number of "
"layers increases, both with and without Pauli twirling.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The observable of interest is $\\langle X \\rangle$ on a single qubit, which"
" we plot as a function of the number of repetitions of CNOT pairs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Because we have rotated our basis before measurements, a measurement of "
"$|0\\rangle$ corresponds to the state having been in $|+\\rangle$ just "
"before the final Hadamard gate, and similar for $|1\\rangle$ and "
"$|-\\rangle$. Therefore, our expectation value $\\langle X \\rangle$ can be "
"simply calculated from the counts of $|0\\rangle$ minus the counts of "
"$|1\\rangle$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We select a reasonable number of CNOT layers over which to allow the error "
"to accumulate, build our circuits, then transpile them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Keep in mind that each layer consists of more than one two-qubit gate. "
"Monitor the transpiled, two-qubit depth by using the function below.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Now we run the jobs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Finally, we visualize these results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-"
"quantum-advantage/error-suppression/extracted-outputs/abf5ce16-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We can clearly see that the twirled circuit yields an expectation value "
"closer to the ideal $\\langle X \\rangle = 1$. This example served its "
"purpose, but let's move onto something more useful: twirling in the "
"production of highly entangled states.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Pauli twirling in GHZ state preparation {#pauli-twirling-in-ghz-state-"
"preparation}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The previous example showed a compelling case for Pauli twirling in "
"destroying the coherent accumulation of errors associated with CNOT gates. "
"GHZ state production uses many CNOT gates to produce highly entangled states"
" useful for many quantum computing applications. Let's explore how Pauli "
"twirling helps in this context, with GHZ states of increasing size.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-"
"quantum-advantage/error-suppression/extracted-outputs/25f23671-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now we build our circuits and transpile them. In this case, we don't have "
"any artificially repeated gates that reduce to the identity. So we can allow"
" the pass manager to do a bit more optimizing for us. We'll set it to level "
"three.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We can see that the optimizer mapped our abstract circuit to qubits 123, "
"124, 136, 142, and 143.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-"
"quantum-advantage/error-suppression/extracted-outputs/2100ead4-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To understand why, let's look at a map of the layout of our backend (in this"
" image, `ibm_fez`, but you can do equivalent analyses on any backend).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![A diagram of the layout of qubits on a quantum computer called ibm_fez. It"
" shows how qubits are chosen to minimize swapping of "
"information.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-"
"suppression/ghz-transpile.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We see that the qubits were selected in a chain to minimize swap gates and "
"thus circuit depth. Further, each CZ gate implemented in the circuit is "
"between adjacent qubits. Finally, all five qubits have relatively low error "
"rates, including readout-assignment error rates. You can check these error "
"rates on any backend on the [Compute "
"resources](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/computers) page. Finding such a "
"layout is not difficult for a simple linear chain, but as problems become "
"more complex, the optimization of the circuit layout becomes more difficult "
"and more valuable.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now we configure our Sampler primitive. We turn off other "
"suppression/mitigation tools to focus on Pauli twirling.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Finally, we run our jobs. You can optionally print the job ID numbers for "
"later retrieval.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We extract the counts of each computational basis state measured for all the"
" circuits, both with and without twirling.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We know the ideal distribution of a GHZ state is that in which half the "
"shots return $|0\\rangle^{\\otimes N}$ and the other half return "
"$|1\\rangle^{\\otimes N}$. Build this for comparison.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We now use the Hellinger fidelity as a measure of the quality of our final "
"state.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Finally we plot our results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-"
"quantum-advantage/error-suppression/extracted-outputs/879b4eb0-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The results using Pauli twirling are no better (and even slightly worse) "
"than without twirling. What happened?\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Two things happened. First, Pauli twirling does not reduce the total amount "
"of noise &mdash; rather, it reshapes coherent, systematic errors into "
"stochastic Pauli‑type errors, so that error growth becomes predictable and "
"modelable. There was never any promise of error reduction, except in special"
" cases.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Secondly, in GHZ circuits, some coherent errors can partially cancel or act "
"like benign phase shifts because of the symmetry of the GHZ construction; "
"twirling removes this accidental protection and replaces it with "
"uncorrelated stochastic Pauli noise, so GHZ fidelity becomes slightly worse "
"under twirling.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This second claim requires some explanation. The claim is not that GHZ "
"circuits are protected from __all__ kinds of coherent error accumulation, "
"only some kinds &mdash; and that in those cases the protection is destroyed "
"by twirling. Specifically, let's consider coherent over-rotation associated "
"with the CX gates. Let us call the real CX gate with over-rotation "
"$\\tilde{CX}$:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\tilde{CX}\\equiv e^{-i\\epsilon K} \\cdot CX\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"where $K$ is any product of Pauli operators, like $X$, $Z$, $Z \\otimes X$, "
"$Z^{\\otimes N}$, etc. For general states, any of these over-rotations could"
" affect measurement statistics (and thus measures of state fidelity). A "
"subset of these, however, leaves many standard GHZ observables unchanged, "
"including operators such as $Z_i Z_j$ and $Z^{\\otimes N}$. In the context "
"of preparing a GHZ state, the relevant over-rotation errors of this type "
"would be:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\tilde{CX}\\equiv e^{-i\\epsilon Z_c Z_t} \\cdot CX\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The preparation of the entire $N$-qubit GHZ state would look like:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\psi_\\text{GHZ}\\rangle=\\prod_{j=0}^{N-2}{e^{-i\\epsilon Z_j Z_{j+1}} \\cdot CX_{j,j+1}}H^{\\otimes N}|0^{\\otimes N}\\rangle\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "After preparing the GHZ state, the state is ideally:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\text{GHZ}^{\\otimes N}\\rangle=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left(|0\\dots 0\\rangle + |1\\dots 1\\rangle\\right).\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This state is a simultaneous eigenstate of a large set of Pauli operators, "
"including the following:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"Z_i Z_j \\quad \\text{for all } i \\neq j,\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"with eigenvalue $+1$. As a result, an operator of the form $e^{-i\\epsilon Z_i Z_j}$ acts on the GHZ state as multiplication by the phase factor $e^{-i\\epsilon}$, which does not affect standard GHZ observables such as parity, collective $X^{\\otimes N}$, or computational‑basis populations.\n"
"Thus, although the over‑rotation errors $e^{-i\\epsilon Z_j Z_{j+1}}$ are coherent and systematic, they are effectively invisible to the measurements used to assess GHZ fidelity. In this sense, the GHZ circuit enjoys an accidental coherence protection: certain coherent CX errors commute with the structure of the state being prepared and therefore do not degrade measured performance.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Pauli twirling fundamentally changes this situation. Twirling does not "
"preserve the coherent over‑rotation error as a deterministic $Z_jZ_{j+1}$ "
"process. Instead, it converts the coherent error channel into an effective "
"stochastic Pauli channel. As a result, the error channel now includes terms "
"such as $X$, $Y$, $X \\otimes Z$, and $Y \\otimes X$, which do not commute "
"with the GHZ stabilizers.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"When these stochastic Pauli errors occur, they create real bit‑flip and "
"phase‑flip faults on individual qubits or pairs of qubits. These errors take"
" the state out of the GHZ stabilizer subspace, reduce interference between "
"$|0\\dots 0\\rangle$ and $|1\\dots 1\\rangle$, and directly lower GHZ "
"fidelity and multi‑qubit parity signals. In other words, Pauli twirling "
"removes the coherent structure of the error but also removes the "
"symmetry‑based cancellation that previously made those errors benign. The "
"result is a slightly worse GHZ state &mdash; not because twirling adds "
"noise, but because it converts a mostly harmless coherent error into "
"genuinely damaging stochastic errors.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This example highlights an important lesson: Pauli twirling is not a "
"universal improvement strategy. It is most beneficial when coherent errors "
"accumulate across a circuit in a manner destructive to the required "
"fidelity. In highly symmetric circuits like GHZ state preparation, some "
"coherent errors are naturally aligned with the state’s stabilizers, and "
"deliberately randomizing them can eliminate this accidental protection.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Combine methods {#combine-methods}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"One can in principle combine Pauli twirling with dynamical decoupling. We "
"have not used DD in this case for two reasons: firstly there should not be "
"any extremely long idle periods in this circuit. Secondly, GHZ states do not"
" store most of their information in single-qubit phase coherence, "
"particularly if we are observing the fidelity only in terms of bitstring "
"counts, and not in terms of the phase between the desired bitstrings "
"$|0\\rangle^{\\otimes N}$ and $|1\\rangle^{\\otimes N}$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If we were to extend the GHZ preparation to 100+ qubits, then the delays in "
"measuring the first/early qubits might indeed be long enough that DD could "
"be useful. This is especially true when we take into account the transpiled "
"depth of the circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Pauli twirling in RTZ echo structure {#pauli-twirling-in-rtz-echo-structure}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Our final example of Pauli twirling in the context of reducing coherent "
"error accumulation uses a circuit with layers of X-CZ-X-CZ combinations. "
"This is a well-known gate sequence that is often used to cancel coherent ZZ "
"error terms in two-qubit gates, even without twirling. But with twirling, we"
" can reduce or randomize other forms of coherent error accumulation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We'll start by defining a function to build the RTZ-like circuits with a "
"varying number of layers.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now we build circuits with increasing layers, up to some reasonable total "
"transpiled two-qubit depth.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-"
"quantum-advantage/error-suppression/extracted-outputs/7027c76d-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"At the high end, some of these are quite deep. Let's use the AerSimulator "
"with no noise model to obtain the ideal states at the end of each of these "
"circuits. We can then can benchmark our results from real quantum computers "
"using the Hellinger fidelity.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Now we define a SamplerV2 with twirling, and one without twirling.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Now we run our jobs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "We get the counts from each of the runs on a real quantum computer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now we find the Hellinger fidelity by comparing each of these runs to the "
"noise-free AerSimulator results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Now we visualize our results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-"
"quantum-advantage/error-suppression/extracted-outputs/548a0f1c-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Throughout this lesson, we have examined cases in which Pauli twirling is "
"used to limit coherent error accumulation, a context in which it might be "
"described as *error suppression*. However, Pauli twirling is often more "
"useful as a tool for reshaping error behavior, converting coherent errors "
"into a form that is more predictable and easier to model. The usefulness of "
